WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.470 --> 00:00:05.120
Independence Day: Resurgence was supposed
to kickstart a whole new franchise in 2016,

00:00:05.120 --> 00:00:08.850
but a third movie hasn't materialized since
then - and it likely never will.

00:00:08.850 --> 00:00:12.440
Here's why the Independence Day series is
finished for good.

00:00:12.480 --> 00:00:17.440
While boasting an ensemble cast of familiar
faces like Jeff Goldblum and Bill Pullman,

00:00:17.440 --> 00:00:20.040
the breakout star of Independence Day was
Will Smith.

00:00:23.280 --> 00:00:25.000
"Welcome to Earth."

00:00:25.000 --> 00:00:29.000
Up to that point, Smith was best known for
his recording career and TV's Fresh Prince

00:00:29.029 --> 00:00:32.750
of Bel-Air, but this movie established him
as a bona fide movie star.

00:00:32.750 --> 00:00:37.190
For years afterwards, Smith virtually owned
Independence Day weekend at the box office,

00:00:37.190 --> 00:00:41.760
starring in a string of early July blockbusters
that included Men in Black, Bad Boys II, and

00:00:41.760 --> 00:00:42.760
Hancock.

00:00:42.760 --> 00:00:46.680
By the time Independence Day: Resurgence began
production nearly 20 years later, Smith was

00:00:46.680 --> 00:00:50.260
too busy to return to the franchise that helped
make him such a screen idol.

00:00:50.260 --> 00:00:58.760
"I had a couple of films lined up, I had Concussion
and Suicide Squad, and so it was a decision,

00:00:58.760 --> 00:01:01.980
timing-wise, between Independence Day and
Suicide Squad."

00:01:01.980 --> 00:01:04.510
In retrospect, that was kind of a no-win scenario.

00:01:04.510 --> 00:01:08.920
Resurgence's screenwriters revised the film's
script to explain the absence of Smith's character,

00:01:08.920 --> 00:01:12.550
Captain Steven Hiller, killing him off screen
before the events of the sequel.

00:01:12.550 --> 00:01:16.740
That creative decision effectively precludes
Smith from returning for any future movies,

00:01:16.740 --> 00:01:20.200
and without its biggest, most bankable star,
why keep the franchise going?

00:01:20.200 --> 00:01:21.979
"Y'all take care, all right?

00:01:21.979 --> 00:01:24.480
Nothin' but love for you."

00:01:24.480 --> 00:01:28.280
When a movie makes a ton of money at the box
office, it makes sense to make a sequel in

00:01:28.300 --> 00:01:31.680
a hurry, while audiences' fond memories of
the movie are fresh.

00:01:31.680 --> 00:01:33.420
This didn't happen with Independence Day.

00:01:33.420 --> 00:01:38.060
The first film was the top grosser of 1996,
raking in $306 million.

00:01:38.060 --> 00:01:43.700
But the sequel hit theaters in 2016, a full
20 years later, barely clearing $100 million

00:01:43.700 --> 00:01:49.170
at the American box office against a reported
production cost of $165 million.

00:01:49.170 --> 00:01:52.960
Making things worse, the people that saw Resurgence
generally didn't care for it.

00:01:52.960 --> 00:01:57.190
It amassed a Rotten Tomatoes audience score
of just 30 percent, slightly better than its

00:01:57.190 --> 00:01:59.200
29 percent rating from critics.

00:01:59.200 --> 00:02:03.550
A review in Entertainment Weekly gave the
film an "F," calling it, quote, "disposable

00:02:03.550 --> 00:02:04.700
and shockingly inept."

00:02:04.700 --> 00:02:09.619
In the end, Fox lost too much money and audience
goodwill on Resurgence to proceed with a sequel,

00:02:09.619 --> 00:02:12.040
getting the message that people weren't interested.

00:02:12.040 --> 00:02:16.880
The original Independence Day is full of talented
actors, but aside from Will Smith, it's not

00:02:16.890 --> 00:02:18.930
exactly a roster of movie stars.

00:02:18.930 --> 00:02:23.480
Actors like Bill Pullman, Randy Quaid, Brent
Spiner, and Vivica A. Fox make for a deep

00:02:23.480 --> 00:02:27.250
bench in these movies' casts, but on their
own, none of them can really carry a big-budget

00:02:27.250 --> 00:02:29.960
sci-fi extravaganza - not even Jeff Goldblum.

00:02:30.000 --> 00:02:31.880
"...We never had a chance."

00:02:31.880 --> 00:02:35.840
While they make for a strong ensemble in the
original movie, these actors have mostly faded

00:02:35.860 --> 00:02:38.770
from the limelight since the first Independence
Day was released.

00:02:38.770 --> 00:02:42.900
The new young additions to the cast also failed
to fill the gap in star power - including

00:02:42.900 --> 00:02:46.910
Liam Hemsworth, best known for his supporting
work in The Hunger Games, and for being related

00:02:46.910 --> 00:02:47.910
to Thor.

00:02:47.910 --> 00:02:51.610
Unless the producers wanted to make the third
movie a full reboot, that's the cast the movie

00:02:51.610 --> 00:02:56.050
would have to work with, and as the box office
returns for Resurgence make clear, audiences

00:02:56.050 --> 00:02:57.700
aren't eager to show up to see them.

00:02:57.700 --> 00:03:01.400
With or without the original cast, another
movie would be a big risk.

00:03:01.400 --> 00:03:06.440
Independence Day came to fruition thanks to
a team up between filmmakers Dean Devlin and

00:03:06.459 --> 00:03:07.490
Roland Emmerich.

00:03:07.490 --> 00:03:11.349
The duo co-wrote the script, with Devlin producing
and Emmerich directing.

00:03:11.349 --> 00:03:14.849
Both returned for Resurgence, but it doesn't
look like that will happen again - at least

00:03:14.849 --> 00:03:15.849
not for Devlin.

00:03:15.849 --> 00:03:19.670
During an appearance at WonderCon in 2018,
the filmmaker was quoted as saying,

00:03:19.670 --> 00:03:22.690
"Currently, I personally have no plans on
doing another one."

00:03:22.690 --> 00:03:26.849
A few weeks later, he solidified his departure
on The Matthew Aaron Show podcast, saying

00:03:26.849 --> 00:03:31.420
he was backing away from working with major
studios after the failure of the 2017 movie

00:03:31.420 --> 00:03:32.420
Geostorm.

00:03:32.420 --> 00:03:36.200
As a result, he excused himself from both
more Independence Day and a reboot of his

00:03:36.200 --> 00:03:38.129
other big franchise, Stargate.

00:03:38.129 --> 00:03:42.019
With one of the chief minds behind the movies
now out of the running, the future of Independence

00:03:42.019 --> 00:03:45.000
Day seems unstable, if not nonexistent.

00:03:45.000 --> 00:03:49.240
The first two Independence Day films were
productions from Twentieth Century Fox - a

00:03:49.260 --> 00:03:54.650
studio which was swallowed up in a $71.3 billion
acquisition by Disney in early 2019.

00:03:54.650 --> 00:03:58.980
That means Disney is now calling the shots
behind the scenes, in total control of that

00:03:58.980 --> 00:04:03.700
studio's old franchises, including Avatar,
Deadpool, and Independence Day.

00:04:03.700 --> 00:04:07.069
Two of those are billion-dollar franchises,
and one… isn't.

00:04:07.069 --> 00:04:10.560
According to The Wall Street Journal, the
new bosses are significantly ramping down

00:04:10.560 --> 00:04:15.420
Fox's movie production, especially when it
comes to expensive projects that aren't family-friendly

00:04:15.420 --> 00:04:17.040
or guaranteed franchise builders.

00:04:17.040 --> 00:04:20.600
That spells bad news for another visit to
this particular well.

00:04:20.600 --> 00:04:23.900
Disney's having a hard enough time keeping
interest in Star Wars going, and that's one

00:04:23.900 --> 00:04:26.030
of the most popular franchises of all time.

00:04:26.030 --> 00:04:29.150
Do you really think they're going to sink
any work into a risky third installment of

00:04:29.150 --> 00:04:30.540
the Independence Day series?

00:04:30.540 --> 00:04:33.690
In the absence of viewers demanding it, it
looks like there's no chance.

00:04:33.690 --> 00:04:37.490
"We will not go quietly into the night!

00:04:37.490 --> 00:04:41.240
We will not vanish without a fight!

00:04:41.240 --> 00:04:43.520
We're going to live on!

00:04:43.520 --> 00:04:45.680
We're going to survive!"

00:04:45.680 --> 00:04:47.350
Yeah… actually?

00:04:47.350 --> 00:04:48.350
Maybe not.

